# Issues and the board: gh dojo

This project tracks the issues of Trum-ok/gh-dojo on the GitHub Projects board of Trum-ok, project 5, with the process in `.github/dojo.toml` (preset `kanban`). Take, move, comment on, block and create issues **only** through `gh dojo`: never change a status, a comment, a blocked-by link or an issue with `gh issue`, `gh project` or the API. For what gh dojo cannot do, such as editing an issue's title, body, labels or assignees, use `gh issue` only when the task needs it or the user asks.

Everything below is enough for everyday work; read `gh dojo <command> --help` only when a command refuses or you need a flag not named here. When the setup is broken (token, board, config), `gh dojo doctor` names each problem with the command that fixes it.

A bare `12` or `#12` is an issue of `Trum-ok/gh-dojo`; an issue of another repository is `owner/repo#12` or its URL.

## Lifecycle

`Backlog` → `In progress` → (`Waiting`) → (`Ready`) → `Done`, through `start`, `wait`, `ready`, `done`. A status in parentheses may be skipped. Each verb below says which statuses it moves from and to.

- `Backlog` is the initial status: `gh dojo new` puts new issues there, and an issue without a status or not on the board counts as `Backlog`.
- Final: `Done`. `gh dojo list` hides issues there unless `--all`.
- A blocker is resolved, no longer blocking, when it is closed or its board status is `Ready` or `Done`: its work is done though the issue may be open.
- A blocker whose status could not be read (`status not read: …`) counts as unresolved.

## Verbs

Run a verb as `gh dojo <verb> N` (`N`, `#N` or `owner/repo#N`); `--dry-run` prints its plan (status, links, the comment it would post) without changing anything. A verb adds the issue to the board if it is missing and refuses a closed issue unless noted. It confirms in one line, such as `#42 Backlog → In progress, comment left`: there is no need to read the issue again.

A verb that takes several numbers at once checks and writes each issue on its own, skips one that fails and names each issue not done in a summary; the same command again touches only what is left.

### `start`

**When:** You begin working on the issue

`gh dojo start N`: Backlog, Waiting, Ready → In progress; optional --comment; comments "Started. Session: {session} {comment}".

- With an unresolved blocker it warns on stderr and still runs: read `gh dojo show N` and decide whether to take the issue, or ask the user.

### `wait`

**When:** Work is stuck: waiting for another issue or an outside decision

`gh dojo wait N`: In progress → Waiting; needs --by or --comment; comments "Waiting for {by}. {comment}".

- `--by 12` (also `#12` or an issue's URL; several comma-separated, `--by 12,13`) also marks the issue blocked by #12. Text (`--by "the user's decision"`) only goes into the comment; issues mixed with text are an error (exit code 1); a pull request is named in the comment without a link. Once the blocker is resolved, go on with `start` or `ready`.

### `ready`

**When:** The work is done, verified and committed, but not released yet

`gh dojo ready N`: In progress, Waiting → Ready; optional --comment; comments "{comment}".

- It comments only when given `--comment`.

### `done`

**When:** The change is released

`gh dojo done N`: Ready, In progress → Done; optional --comment; also on closed issues; comments "{comment}"; closes as completed.

- It takes several numbers at once: `gh dojo done 12 13 14`.
- For a release, `gh dojo release <tag> --verb done --dry-run` lists the issues the release includes and its plan; read `gh dojo release --help` before you run it.
- It comments only when given `--comment`.
- An issue closed already stays closed as it is.

### `drop`

**When:** задача не нужна или дубль

`gh dojo drop N`: any status → status unchanged; needs --comment; on a closed issue only if closed as not planned; comments "{comment}"; closes as not planned.

- It is the user's decision: without `--yes` it exits with code 2. Ask the user, and run it again with `--yes` only if they agree; never add `--yes` on your own.
- An issue closed already stays closed as it is.

### `review`

**When:** A pull request waits for review

`gh dojo review N`: In progress, Ready → In review; optional --comment; labels +enhancement -dogfood; assigns you; comments "{comment}".

- It comments only when given `--comment`.

### `revive`

**When:** A closed issue is needed after all

`gh dojo revive N`: any status → Backlog; also on closed issues; reopens.

### `tag`

**When:** Tag it

`gh dojo tag N`: any status → status unchanged; labels +enhancment.

### `give`

**When:** Hand it over

`gh dojo give N`: any status → status unchanged; assigns octo-nobody.

### `recase`

**When:** Labels, one in another case than the repository's

`gh dojo recase N`: any status → status unchanged; labels +enhancement -Dogfood.

### `tagcase`

**When:** Tag it, in another case than the repository's

`gh dojo tagcase N`: any status → status unchanged; labels +Enhancement.

### `untag`

**When:** Remove a label the repository does not have

`gh dojo untag N`: any status → status unchanged; labels -no-such-label.

### `hand`

**When:** Hand it to the owner, login in lowercase

`gh dojo hand N`: any status → status unchanged; assigns trum-ok.

### `plan`

**When:** The issue moves to another stage

`gh dojo plan N`: any status → status unchanged; needs --milestone; sets the --milestone milestone; comments "Moved to {milestone}.".

- `--milestone <title>` names the open milestone to put the issue in, by its title or a start only that title has (`--milestone v1.2`); none or several matching is exit code 1, with the open milestones or the candidates. Ask the user which milestone if you do not know.
- It takes several numbers at once: `gh dojo plan 12 13 14`.

### `ship`

**When:** Due in the agent release

`gh dojo ship N`: any status → status unchanged; sets milestone v0.2.

### `ship-later`

**When:** Due in a release without a milestone yet

`gh dojo ship-later N`: any status → status unchanged; sets milestone v9.

## Reading

- `gh dojo next`: what to take next, the open issues in `Backlog` or without a status, with no unresolved blocker, in board order, 5 by default (`--limit N`). The blocked ones are hidden and counted; `--all` shows them with their blockers. Filters: `--mine`, `--label <label>`. Once you pick one, `gh dojo show N`, then `gh dojo start N`.
- `gh dojo show N` before you work on an issue: its status, milestone, labels, blockers both ways (unresolved first, each with its board status), its parent and sub-issues (the same way), status history, description and the last 5 comments; a line left out means none. Several numbers, `gh dojo show 12 13`, print three lines per issue: read the one you take alone. `--comments all` prints the whole thread (up to 100), `--full` everything uncut.
- `gh dojo list`: an overview of the board, when the user asks for one: the open issues grouped by status; closed issues and final statuses are hidden and counted. Filters: `--status "<status>"`, `--status none`, `--mine`, `--label <label>`, `--all`, `--limit 100`.

## Creating and linking issues

- `gh dojo new "<title>" --type <label> --body "…"`: the issue, its labels, the board in `Backlog`, in one command. A long body: `--body-file <file>`, or `--body-file -` for stdin. More labels: `--label X`. `--area X` adds the label `area:X`. `--milestone "<title>"`: an open milestone, by its title or a start only that title has (none or several is exit code 1 with the list). `--blocked-by 12`: blocked from the start (repeat it, or comma-separate; an issue that does not exist is exit code 3, and nothing is created). `--parent 12`: a sub-issue of #12 (one issue; one that does not exist is exit code 3, and nothing is created). A title starting with `-` goes after `--`, and every flag before it: `gh dojo new --label bug -- "-v is ignored"`.
- `gh dojo comment N "text"`, or `gh dojo comment N --body-file -`: a comment without changing the status.
- `gh dojo block N --by M` marks N blocked by M without changing the status; `gh dojo unblock N --by M` removes that. `--by` repeats or takes a comma-separated list. A link already there (or already gone) is not an error: the line says `already`. A pull request cannot block (exit code 3), nor can an issue N blocks (a cycle, exit code 1).
- An existing issue becomes a sub-issue with `gh issue edit N --parent M` (`--remove-parent` undoes it): gh-dojo has no command for it, since the link changes no status.

## Repeats are safe

Run the same command again after an error: a verb does not set a status that is already set, nor post a comment that is already the latest from gh-dojo; `comment` does not post its last comment again; `block` and `unblock` skip links that are already as asked; `new` with the same title finishes the issue it created instead of creating another.

## Session

`{session}` in a comment is a link to your agent session, from `DOJO_SESSION` (the first one set). When none is set the verb warns and still runs.

## Exit codes

On an error, read its `error:`, `allowed:` and `fix:` lines and do what `fix:` says. Exit code 2 is a process check refusing: never work around it (do not move the status another way); ask the user. On 4 (network, rate limit or auth) and 5 (partially applied), run the same command again, on 4 a bit later.

## JSON

Every command takes `--json`, `--jq '<expression>'` and `--template`, as gh does; an error is then JSON too (`error` with `exit_code`, `message`, `allowed`, `fix`), with the same exit code. Use it to pick values out of the output; for everyday work the text is shorter.

```sh
gh dojo list --jq '.issues[] | select(.status == "Ready") | .number'
gh dojo show 42 --jq '.issues[0].blocked_by[] | select(.resolved | not) | .ref'
```
